1
00:00:03,065 --> 00:00:07,799
COLONEL FITZWILLIAM’S manners were very much admired at the Parsonage, and the ladies all

2
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:12,765
felt that he must add considerably to the pleasure of their engagements at Rosings. It was some days,

3
00:00:12,765 --> 00:00:16,433
however, before they received any invitation thither, for while there

4
00:00:16,433 --> 00:00:20,866
were visitors in the house they could not be necessary; and it was not till Easter-day,

5
00:00:20,866 --> 00:00:25,065
almost a week after the gentlemen’s arrival, that they were honoured by such an attention,

6
00:00:25,065 --> 00:00:29,933
and then they were merely asked on leaving church to come there in the evening. For the

7
00:00:29,933 --> 00:00:34,200
last week they had seen very little of either Lady Catherine or her daughter. Colonel Fitzwilliam had

8
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:38,700
called at the Parsonage more than once during the time, but Mr. Darcy they had only seen at church.

9
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:44,099
The invitation was accepted, of course, and at a proper hour they joined the party in

10
00:00:44,099 --> 00:00:48,900
Lady Catherine’s drawing-room. Her Ladyship received them civilly, but it was plain that

11
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:53,400
their company was by no means so acceptable as when she could get nobody else; and she was,

12
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:58,200
in fact, almost engrossed by her nephews, speaking to them, especially to Darcy,

13
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:03,233
much more than to any other person in the room. Colonel Fitzwilliam seemed really glad to see

14
00:01:03,233 --> 00:01:07,866
them: anything was a welcome relief to him at Rosings; and Mrs. Collins’s pretty friend had,

15
00:01:07,866 --> 00:01:13,200
moreover, caught his fancy very much. He now seated himself by her, and talked so agreeably

16
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:17,765
of Kent and Hertfordshire, of travelling and staying at home, of new books and music,

17
00:01:17,765 --> 00:01:21,099
that Elizabeth had never been half so well entertained in that room before;

18
00:01:21,099 --> 00:01:26,265
and they conversed with so much spirit and flow as to draw the attention of Lady Catherine herself,

19
00:01:26,265 --> 00:01:32,032
as well as of Mr. Darcy. His eyes had been soon and repeatedly turned towards them with a look of

20
00:01:32,032 --> 00:01:37,065
curiosity; and that her Ladyship, after a while, shared the feeling, was more openly acknowledged,

21
00:01:37,065 --> 00:01:42,065
for she did not scruple to call out,— “What is that you are saying, Fitzwilliam?

22
00:01:42,065 --> 00:01:47,265
What is it you are talking of? What are you telling Miss Bennet? Let me hear what it is.”

23
00:01:48,233 --> 00:01:53,332
“We were talking of music, madam,” said he, when no longer able to avoid a reply.

24
00:01:53,933 --> 00:02:02,265
“Of music! Then pray speak aloud. It is of all subjects my delight. I must have my share in the

25
00:02:02,265 --> 00:02:07,200
conversation, if you are speaking of music. There are few people in England, I suppose,

26
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:14,500
who have more true enjoyment of music than myself, or a better natural taste. If I had ever learnt, I

27
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:20,200
should have been a great proficient. And so would Anne, if her health had allowed her to apply.

28
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:26,166
I am confident that she would have performed delightfully. How does Georgiana get on, Darcy?”

29
00:02:26,765 --> 00:02:30,233
Mr. Darcy spoke with affectionate praise of his sister’s proficiency.

30
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,065
“I am very glad to hear such a good account of her,” said Lady Catherine; “and

31
00:02:36,065 --> 00:02:41,565
pray tell her from me, that she cannot expect to excel, if she does not practise a great deal.”

32
00:02:42,466 --> 00:02:44,565
“I assure you, madam,” he replied,

33
00:02:44,565 --> 00:02:48,466
“that she does not need such advice. She practises very constantly.”

34
00:02:49,565 --> 00:02:55,900
“So much the better. It cannot be done too much; and when I next write to her, I shall charge her

35
00:02:55,900 --> 00:03:01,866
not to neglect it on any account. I often tell young ladies, that no excellence in music is to

36
00:03:01,866 --> 00:03:07,433
be acquired without constant practice. I have told Miss Bennet several times, that she will

37
00:03:07,433 --> 00:03:13,299
never play really well, unless she practises more; and though Mrs. Collins has no instrument, she is

38
00:03:13,299 --> 00:03:19,133
very welcome, as I have often told her, to come to Rosings every day, and play on the pianoforte in

39
00:03:19,133 --> 00:03:23,265
Mrs. Jenkinson’s room. She would be in nobody’s way, you know, in that part of the house.”

40
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,265
Mr. Darcy looked a little ashamed of his aunt’s ill-breeding, and made no answer.

41
00:03:29,032 --> 00:03:33,400
When coffee was over, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth of having promised to play to him;

42
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:39,065
and she sat down directly to the instrument. He drew a chair near her. Lady Catherine listened

43
00:03:39,065 --> 00:03:43,966
to half a song, and then talked, as before, to her other nephew; till the latter walked away

44
00:03:43,966 --> 00:03:49,133
from her, and moving with his usual deliberation towards the pianoforte, stationed himself so as

45
00:03:49,133 --> 00:03:53,700
to command a full view of the fair performer’s countenance. Elizabeth saw what he was doing,

46
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:58,065
and at the first convenient pause turned to him with an arch smile, and said,—

47
00:03:58,666 --> 00:04:03,366
“You mean to frighten me, Mr. Darcy, by coming in all this state to hear me. But I

48
00:04:03,366 --> 00:04:07,666
will not be alarmed, though your sister does play so well. There is a stubbornness about

49
00:04:07,666 --> 00:04:11,565
me that never can bear to be frightened at the will of others. My courage always

50
00:04:11,565 --> 00:04:17,332
rises with every attempt to intimidate me.” “I shall not say that you are mistaken,” he

51
00:04:17,332 --> 00:04:21,666
replied, “because you could not really believe me to entertain any design of alarming you;

52
00:04:21,666 --> 00:04:25,565
and I have had the pleasure of your acquaintance long enough to know, that you find great enjoyment

53
00:04:25,565 --> 00:04:28,900
in occasionally professing opinions which, in fact, are not your own.”

54
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:33,832
Elizabeth laughed heartily at this picture of herself, and said to Colonel Fitzwilliam,

55
00:04:33,832 --> 00:04:39,233
“Your cousin will give you a very pretty notion of me, and teach you not to believe a word I

56
00:04:39,233 --> 00:04:45,000
say. I am particularly unlucky in meeting with a person so well able to expose my real character,

57
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,400
in a part of the world where I had hoped to pass myself off with some degree of credit. Indeed,

58
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:54,966
Mr. Darcy, it is very ungenerous in you to mention all that you knew to my disadvantage

59
00:04:54,966 --> 00:05:00,900
in Hertfordshire—and, give me leave to say, very impolitic too—for it is provoking me to retaliate,

60
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:04,599
and such things may come out as will shock your relations to hear.”

61
00:05:05,332 --> 00:05:11,032
“I am not afraid of you,” said he, smilingly. “Pray let me hear what you have to accuse

62
00:05:11,032 --> 00:05:15,832
him of,” cried Colonel Fitzwilliam. “I should like to know how he behaves among strangers.”

63
00:05:16,599 --> 00:05:21,966
“You shall hear, then—but prepare for something very dreadful. The first time

64
00:05:21,966 --> 00:05:26,265
of my ever seeing him in Hertfordshire, you must know, was at a ball—and at this ball,

65
00:05:26,265 --> 00:05:29,765
what do you think he did? He danced only four dances! I am sorry to pain you, but so it was.

66
00:05:29,765 --> 00:05:33,700
He danced only four dances, though gentlemen were scarce; and, to my certain knowledge, more than

67
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:38,866
one young lady was sitting down in want of a partner. Mr. Darcy, you cannot deny the fact.”

68
00:05:39,666 --> 00:05:44,032
“I had not at that time the honour of knowing any lady in the assembly beyond my own party.”

69
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:50,633
“True; and nobody can ever be introduced in a ball-room. Well, Colonel Fitzwilliam,

70
00:05:50,633 --> 00:05:57,532
what do I play next? My fingers wait your orders.” “Perhaps,” said Darcy, “I should have judged

71
00:05:57,532 --> 00:06:03,099
better had I sought an introduction, but I am ill-qualified to recommend myself to strangers.”

72
00:06:04,265 --> 00:06:08,633
“Shall we ask your cousin the reason of this?” said Elizabeth, still addressing Colonel

73
00:06:08,633 --> 00:06:14,565
Fitzwilliam. “Shall we ask him why a man of sense and education, and who has lived in the world, is

74
00:06:14,565 --> 00:06:20,866
ill-qualified to recommend himself to strangers?” “I can answer your question,” said Fitzwilliam,

75
00:06:20,866 --> 00:06:24,900
“without applying to him. It is because he will not give himself the trouble.”

76
00:06:25,666 --> 00:06:31,500
“I certainly have not the talent which some people possess,” said Darcy, “of conversing easily with

77
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:37,200
those I have never seen before. I cannot catch their tone of conversation, or appear interested

78
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:43,366
in their concerns, as I often see done.” “My fingers,” said Elizabeth, “do not move

79
00:06:43,366 --> 00:06:48,233
over this instrument in the masterly manner which I see so many women’s do. They have not the same

80
00:06:48,233 --> 00:06:53,799
force or rapidity, and do not produce the same expression. But then I have always supposed it

81
00:06:53,799 --> 00:06:58,366
to be my own fault—because I would not take the trouble of practising. It is not that

82
00:06:58,366 --> 00:07:02,265
I do not believe my fingers as capable as any other woman’s of superior execution.”

83
00:07:03,233 --> 00:07:08,332
Darcy smiled and said, “You are perfectly right. You have employed your time much

84
00:07:08,332 --> 00:07:12,700
better. No one admitted to the privilege of hearing you can think anything wanting.

85
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:18,900
We neither of us perform to strangers.” Here they were interrupted by Lady Catherine,

86
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:23,200
who called out to know what they were talking of. Elizabeth immediately began playing

87
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:28,733
again. Lady Catherine approached, and, after listening for a few minutes, said to Darcy,—

88
00:07:28,733 --> 00:07:34,065
“Miss Bennet would not play at all amiss if she practised more, and could have the advantage of

89
00:07:34,065 --> 00:07:38,700
a London master. She has a very good notion of fingering, though her taste is not equal

90
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:44,032
to Anne’s. Anne would have been a delightful performer, had her health allowed her to learn.”

91
00:07:45,166 --> 00:07:49,000
Elizabeth looked at Darcy, to see how cordially he assented to his cousin’s praise:

92
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:53,466
but neither at that moment nor at any other could she discern any symptom of love;

93
00:07:53,466 --> 00:07:58,299
and from the whole of his behaviour to Miss De Bourgh she derived this comfort for Miss Bingley,

94
00:07:58,299 --> 00:08:02,466
that he might have been just as likely to marry her, had she been his relation.

95
00:08:03,366 --> 00:08:06,400
Lady Catherine continued her remarks on Elizabeth’s performance,

96
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:11,332
mixing with them many instructions on execution and taste. Elizabeth received

97
00:08:11,332 --> 00:08:15,599
them with all the forbearance of civility; and at the request of the gentlemen remained

98
00:08:15,599 --> 00:08:18,666
at the instrument till her Ladyship’s carriage was ready to take them all home.

99
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:22,000
CHAPTER XXXII.

100
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:31,900
ELIZABETH was sitting by herself the next morning, and writing to Jane,

101
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:35,799
while Mrs. Collins and Maria were gone on business into the village, when she

102
00:08:35,799 --> 00:08:40,900
was startled by a ring at the door, the certain signal of a visitor. As she had heard no carriage,

103
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:46,265
she thought it not unlikely to be Lady Catherine; and under that apprehension was putting away her

104
00:08:46,265 --> 00:08:50,500
half-finished letter, that she might escape all impertinent questions, when the door opened,

105
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:55,599
and to her very great surprise Mr. Darcy, and Mr. Darcy only, entered the room.

106
00:08:56,265 --> 00:09:00,599
He seemed astonished too on finding her alone, and apologized for his intrusion,

107
00:09:00,599 --> 00:09:03,599
by letting her know that he had understood all the ladies to be within.

108
00:09:03,599 --> 00:09:08,866
They then sat down, and when her inquiries after Rosings were made, seemed in danger of

109
00:09:08,866 --> 00:09:13,265
sinking into total silence. It was absolutely necessary, therefore, to think of something;

110
00:09:13,265 --> 00:09:17,633
and in this emergency recollecting when she had seen him last in Hertfordshire, and

111
00:09:17,633 --> 00:09:21,900
feeling curious to know what he would say on the subject of their hasty departure, she observed,—

112
00:09:22,565 --> 00:09:27,299
“How very suddenly you all quitted Netherfield last November, Mr. Darcy! It must have been a

113
00:09:27,299 --> 00:09:32,265
most agreeable surprise to Mr. Bingley to see you all after him so soon; for, if I recollect right,

114
00:09:32,265 --> 00:09:37,200
he went but the day before. He and his sisters were well, I hope, when you left London?”

115
00:09:38,265 --> 00:09:42,832
“Perfectly so, I thank you.” She found that she was to receive no other answer;

116
00:09:42,832 --> 00:09:47,032
and, after a short pause, added,— “I think I have understood that Mr.

117
00:09:47,032 --> 00:09:50,400
Bingley has not much idea of ever returning to Netherfield again?”

118
00:09:51,299 --> 00:09:55,299
“I have never heard him say so; but it is probable that he may spend very little of his

119
00:09:55,299 --> 00:09:59,400
time there in future. He has many friends, and he is at a time of life when friends

120
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:04,366
and engagements are continually increasing.” “If he means to be but little at Netherfield,

121
00:10:04,366 --> 00:10:07,500
it would be better for the neighbourhood that he should give up the place entirely,

122
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:12,700
for then we might possibly get a settled family there. But, perhaps, Mr. Bingley did not take

123
00:10:12,700 --> 00:10:16,733
the house so much for the convenience of the neighbourhood as for his own, and we must expect

124
00:10:16,733 --> 00:10:22,733
him to keep or quit it on the same principle.” “I should not be surprised,” said Darcy,

125
00:10:22,733 --> 00:10:25,666
“if he were to give it up as soon as any eligible purchase offers.”

126
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:31,133
Elizabeth made no answer. She was afraid of talking longer of his friend; and,

127
00:10:31,133 --> 00:10:35,332
having nothing else to say, was now determined to leave the trouble of finding a subject to him.

128
00:10:36,299 --> 00:10:41,933
He took the hint and soon began with, “This seems a very comfortable house. Lady Catherine,

129
00:10:41,933 --> 00:10:45,366
I believe, did a great deal to it when Mr. Collins first came to Hunsford.”

130
00:10:46,133 --> 00:10:49,966
“I believe she did—and I am sure she could not have bestowed her kindness

131
00:10:49,966 --> 00:10:53,933
on a more grateful object.” “Mr. Collins appears very

132
00:10:53,933 --> 00:10:58,200
fortunate in his choice of a wife.” “Yes, indeed; his friends may well

133
00:10:58,200 --> 00:11:01,866
rejoice in his having met with one of the very few sensible women who would have accepted him,

134
00:11:01,866 --> 00:11:07,666
or have made him happy if they had. My friend has an excellent understanding—though I am not certain

135
00:11:07,666 --> 00:11:12,532
that I consider her marrying Mr. Collins as the wisest thing she ever did. She seems perfectly

136
00:11:12,532 --> 00:11:16,565
happy, however; and, in a prudential light, it is certainly a very good match for her.”

137
00:11:17,332 --> 00:11:20,633
“It must be very agreeable to her to be settled within so easy a distance

138
00:11:20,633 --> 00:11:24,233
of her own family and friends.” “An easy distance do you call

139
00:11:24,233 --> 00:11:28,065
it? It is nearly fifty miles.” “And what is fifty miles of good road?

140
00:11:28,065 --> 00:11:31,966
Little more than half a day’s journey. Yes, I call it a very easy distance.”

141
00:11:33,065 --> 00:11:36,599
“I should never have considered the distance as one of the advantages of the match,”

142
00:11:36,599 --> 00:11:41,265
cried Elizabeth. “I should never have said Mrs. Collins was settled near her family.”

143
00:11:42,233 --> 00:11:45,832
“It is a proof of your own attachment to Hertfordshire. Anything beyond the

144
00:11:45,832 --> 00:11:48,466
very neighbourhood of Longbourn, I suppose, would appear far.”

145
00:11:49,299 --> 00:11:52,966
As he spoke there was a sort of smile, which Elizabeth fancied she understood;

146
00:11:52,966 --> 00:11:58,299
he must be supposing her to be thinking of Jane and Netherfield, and she blushed as she answered,—

147
00:11:58,900 --> 00:12:03,599
“I do not mean to say that a woman may not be settled too near her family. The

148
00:12:03,599 --> 00:12:07,799
far and the near must be relative, and depend on many varying circumstances.

149
00:12:07,799 --> 00:12:12,966
Where there is fortune to make the expense of travelling unimportant, distance becomes

150
00:12:12,966 --> 00:12:18,099
no evil. But that is not the case here. Mr. and Mrs. Collins have a comfortable income,

151
00:12:18,099 --> 00:12:23,032
but not such a one as will allow of frequent journeys—and I am persuaded my friend would

152
00:12:23,032 --> 00:12:27,233
not call herself near her family under less than half the present distance.”

153
00:12:28,065 --> 00:12:33,366
Mr. Darcy drew his chair a little towards her, and said, “You cannot have a right to

154
00:12:33,366 --> 00:12:37,866
such very strong local attachment. You cannot have been always at Longbourn.”

155
00:12:38,933 --> 00:12:43,799
Elizabeth looked surprised. The gentleman experienced some change of feeling; he drew

156
00:12:43,799 --> 00:12:48,765
back his chair, took a newspaper from the table, and, glancing over it, said, in a colder voice,—

157
00:12:49,565 --> 00:12:53,933
“Are you pleased with Kent?” A short dialogue on the subject of the country

158
00:12:53,933 --> 00:12:59,765
ensued, on either side calm and concise—and soon put an end to by the entrance of Charlotte and

159
00:12:59,765 --> 00:13:05,400
her sister, just returned from their walk. The tête-à-tête surprised them. Mr. Darcy related the

160
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:09,532
mistake which had occasioned his intruding on Miss Bennet, and, after sitting a few minutes longer,

161
00:13:09,532 --> 00:13:11,866
without saying much to anybody, went away.

162
00:13:12,599 --> 00:13:17,700
“What can be the meaning of this?” said Charlotte, as soon as he was gone. “My dear

163
00:13:17,700 --> 00:13:22,065
Eliza, he must be in love with you, or he would never have called on us in this familiar way.”

164
00:13:22,799 --> 00:13:27,599
But when Elizabeth told of his silence, it did not seem very likely, even to Charlotte’s wishes,

165
00:13:27,599 --> 00:13:32,866
to be the case; and, after various conjectures, they could at last only suppose his visit to

166
00:13:32,866 --> 00:13:37,000
proceed from the difficulty of finding anything to do, which was the more probable from the time

167
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:42,832
of year. All field sports were over. Within doors there was Lady Catherine, books, and a billiard

168
00:13:42,832 --> 00:13:47,565
table, but gentlemen cannot be always within doors; and in the nearness of the Parsonage,

169
00:13:47,565 --> 00:13:52,366
or the pleasantness of the walk to it, or of the people who lived in it, the two cousins found a

170
00:13:52,366 --> 00:13:57,166
temptation from this period of walking thither almost every day. They called at various times

171
00:13:57,166 --> 00:14:01,866
of the morning, sometimes separately, sometimes together, and now and then accompanied by their

172
00:14:01,866 --> 00:14:06,366
aunt. It was plain to them all that Colonel Fitzwilliam came because he had pleasure in their

173
00:14:06,366 --> 00:14:11,400
society, a persuasion which of course recommended him still more; and Elizabeth was reminded by her

174
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:16,599
own satisfaction in being with him, as well as by his evident admiration, of her former favourite,

175
00:14:16,599 --> 00:14:20,633
George Wickham; and though, in comparing them, she saw there was less captivating

176
00:14:20,633 --> 00:14:25,133
softness in Colonel Fitzwilliam’s manners, she believed he might have the best informed mind.

177
00:14:25,733 --> 00:14:31,265
But why Mr. Darcy came so often to the Parsonage it was more difficult to understand. It could

178
00:14:31,265 --> 00:14:35,866
not be for society, as he frequently sat there ten minutes together without opening his lips;

179
00:14:35,866 --> 00:14:40,866
and when he did speak, it seemed the effect of necessity rather than of choice—a sacrifice to

180
00:14:40,866 --> 00:14:47,200
propriety, not a pleasure to himself. He seldom appeared really animated. Mrs. Collins knew not

181
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:51,900
what to make of him. Colonel Fitzwilliam’s occasionally laughing at his stupidity proved

182
00:14:51,900 --> 00:14:55,299
that he was generally different, which her own knowledge of him could not have told her;

183
00:14:55,299 --> 00:15:00,065
and as she would have liked to believe this change the effect of love, and the object of that love

184
00:15:00,065 --> 00:15:05,466
her friend Eliza, she set herself seriously to work to find it out: she watched him whenever they

185
00:15:05,466 --> 00:15:10,666
were at Rosings, and whenever he came to Hunsford; but without much success. He certainly looked at

186
00:15:10,666 --> 00:15:15,366
her friend a great deal, but the expression of that look was disputable. It was an earnest,

187
00:15:15,366 --> 00:15:20,500
steadfast gaze, but she often doubted whether there were much admiration in it, and sometimes

188
00:15:20,500 --> 00:15:25,366
it seemed nothing but absence of mind. She had once or twice suggested to Elizabeth

189
00:15:25,366 --> 00:15:30,466
the possibility of his being partial to her, but Elizabeth always laughed at the idea; and

190
00:15:30,466 --> 00:15:34,900
Mrs. Collins did not think it right to press the subject, from the danger of raising expectations

191
00:15:34,900 --> 00:15:39,466
which might only end in disappointment; for in her opinion it admitted not of a doubt,

192
00:15:39,466 --> 00:15:43,565
that all her friend’s dislike would vanish, if she could suppose him to be in her power.

193
00:15:44,466 --> 00:15:48,599
In her kind schemes for Elizabeth, she sometimes planned her marrying Colonel

194
00:15:48,599 --> 00:15:53,265
Fitzwilliam. He was, beyond comparison, the pleasantest man: he certainly admired her,

195
00:15:53,265 --> 00:15:57,765
and his situation in life was most eligible; but, to counterbalance these advantages,

196
00:15:57,765 --> 00:16:02,799
Mr. Darcy had considerable patronage in the church, and his cousin could have none at all.

197
00:16:04,966 --> 00:16:06,000
CHAPTER XXXIII.

198
00:16:13,133 --> 00:16:19,366
MORE than once did Elizabeth, in her ramble within the park, unexpectedly meet Mr. Darcy. She felt

199
00:16:19,366 --> 00:16:23,400
all the perverseness of the mischance that should bring him where no one else was brought; and,

200
00:16:23,400 --> 00:16:27,900
to prevent its ever happening again, took care to inform him, at first, that it was a favourite

201
00:16:27,900 --> 00:16:33,900
haunt of hers. How it could occur a second time, therefore, was very odd! Yet it did,

202
00:16:33,900 --> 00:16:40,133
and even the third. It seemed like wilful ill-nature, or a voluntary penance; for on

203
00:16:40,133 --> 00:16:44,565
these occasions it was not merely a few formal inquiries and an awkward pause and then away,

204
00:16:44,565 --> 00:16:50,099
but he actually thought it necessary to turn back and walk with her. He never said a great deal,

205
00:16:50,099 --> 00:16:54,900
nor did she give herself the trouble of talking or of listening much; but it struck her in the course

206
00:16:54,900 --> 00:17:00,532
of their third rencounter that he was asking some odd unconnected questions—about her pleasure in

207
00:17:00,532 --> 00:17:05,765
being at Hunsford, her love of solitary walks, and her opinion of Mr. and Mrs. Collins’s happiness;

208
00:17:05,765 --> 00:17:10,733
and that in speaking of Rosings, and her not perfectly understanding the house, he seemed

209
00:17:10,733 --> 00:17:16,133
to expect that whenever she came into Kent again she would be staying there too. His words seemed

210
00:17:16,133 --> 00:17:21,000
to imply it. Could he have Colonel Fitzwilliam in his thoughts? She supposed, if he meant anything,

211
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,565
he must mean an allusion to what might arise in that quarter. It distressed her a little,

212
00:17:25,565 --> 00:17:30,466
and she was quite glad to find herself at the gate in the pales opposite the Parsonage.

213
00:17:30,466 --> 00:17:33,966
She was engaged one day, as she walked, in re-perusing Jane’s last letter,

214
00:17:33,966 --> 00:17:38,633
and dwelling on some passages which proved that Jane had not written in spirits, when,

215
00:17:38,633 --> 00:17:43,065
instead of being again surprised by Mr. Darcy, she saw, on looking up, that Colonel Fitzwilliam

216
00:17:43,065 --> 00:17:47,166
was meeting her. Putting away the letter immediately, and forcing a smile, she said,—

217
00:17:47,765 --> 00:17:50,166
“I did not know before that you ever walked this way.”

218
00:17:50,799 --> 00:17:54,966
“I have been making the tour of the park,” he replied, “as I generally do every year,

219
00:17:54,966 --> 00:17:58,666
and intended to close it with a call at the Parsonage. Are you going much farther?”

220
00:17:59,200 --> 00:18:04,200
“No, I should have turned in a moment.” And accordingly she did turn, and they walked

221
00:18:04,200 --> 00:18:07,966
towards the Parsonage together. “Do you certainly leave Kent

222
00:18:07,966 --> 00:18:11,466
on Saturday?” said she. “Yes—if Darcy does not put

223
00:18:11,466 --> 00:18:15,832
it off again. But I am at his disposal. He arranges the business just as he pleases.”

224
00:18:16,599 --> 00:18:20,765
“And if not able to please himself in the arrangement, he has at least great pleasure

225
00:18:20,765 --> 00:18:25,000
in the power of choice. I do not know anybody who seems more to enjoy the

226
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:30,400
power of doing what he likes than Mr. Darcy.” “He likes to have his own way very well,” replied

227
00:18:30,400 --> 00:18:35,400
Colonel Fitzwilliam. “But so we all do. It is only that he has better means of having it than many

228
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:41,166
others, because he is rich, and many others are poor. I speak feelingly. A younger son, you know,

229
00:18:41,166 --> 00:18:47,032
must be inured to self-denial and dependence.” “In my opinion, the younger son of an earl can

230
00:18:47,032 --> 00:18:52,299
know very little of either. Now, seriously, what have you ever known of self-denial and dependence?

231
00:18:52,299 --> 00:18:56,400
When have you been prevented by want of money from going wherever you chose or

232
00:18:56,400 --> 00:19:01,565
procuring anything you had a fancy for?” “These are home questions—and perhaps I

233
00:19:01,565 --> 00:19:06,233
cannot say that I have experienced many hardships of that nature. But in matters of greater weight,

234
00:19:06,233 --> 00:19:10,000
I may suffer from the want of money. Younger sons cannot marry where they like.”

235
00:19:10,733 --> 00:19:14,500
“Unless where they like women of fortune, which I think they very often do.”

236
00:19:15,099 --> 00:19:19,900
“Our habits of expense make us too dependent, and there are not many in my rank of life who can

237
00:19:19,900 --> 00:19:25,866
afford to marry without some attention to money.” “Is this,” thought Elizabeth, “meant for me?”

238
00:19:26,466 --> 00:19:31,900
and she coloured at the idea; but, recovering herself, said in a lively tone, “And pray,

239
00:19:31,900 --> 00:19:36,966
what is the usual price of an earl’s younger son? Unless the elder brother is very sickly, I suppose

240
00:19:36,966 --> 00:19:41,765
you would not ask above fifty thousand pounds.” He answered her in the same style, and the

241
00:19:41,765 --> 00:19:45,966
subject dropped. To interrupt a silence which might make him fancy her affected

242
00:19:45,966 --> 00:19:51,099
with what had passed, she soon afterwards said,— “I imagine your cousin brought you down with him

243
00:19:51,099 --> 00:19:55,500
chiefly for the sake of having somebody at his disposal. I wonder he does not marry,

244
00:19:55,500 --> 00:20:01,000
to secure a lasting convenience of that kind. But, perhaps, his sister does as well for the present;

245
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:04,666
and, as she is under his sole care, he may do what he likes with her.”

246
00:20:05,332 --> 00:20:09,666
“No,” said Colonel Fitzwilliam, “that is an advantage which he must divide

247
00:20:09,666 --> 00:20:13,366
with me. I am joined with him in the guardianship of Miss Darcy.”

248
00:20:13,366 --> 00:20:18,166
“Are you, indeed? And pray what sort of a guardian do you make? Does your charge give

249
00:20:18,166 --> 00:20:22,000
you much trouble? Young ladies of her age are sometimes a little difficult to manage;

250
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:26,166
and if she has the true Darcy spirit, she may like to have her own way.”

251
00:20:26,866 --> 00:20:31,433
As she spoke, she observed him looking at her earnestly; and the manner in which he

252
00:20:31,433 --> 00:20:35,099
immediately asked her why she supposed Miss Darcy likely to give them any uneasiness,

253
00:20:35,099 --> 00:20:39,832
convinced her that she had somehow or other got pretty near the truth. She directly replied,—

254
00:20:40,666 --> 00:20:44,933
“You need not be frightened. I never heard any harm of her; and I dare say she is one of the

255
00:20:44,933 --> 00:20:48,900
most tractable creatures in the world. She is a very great favourite with some ladies of my

256
00:20:48,900 --> 00:20:53,265
acquaintance, Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley. I think I have heard you say that you know them.”

257
00:20:53,799 --> 00:20:57,700
“I know them a little. Their brother is a pleasant, gentlemanlike man—he

258
00:20:57,700 --> 00:21:02,265
is a great friend of Darcy’s.” “Oh yes,” said Elizabeth drily—“Mr.

259
00:21:02,265 --> 00:21:06,666
Darcy is uncommonly kind to Mr. Bingley, and takes a prodigious deal of care of him.”

260
00:21:07,265 --> 00:21:11,332
“Care of him! Yes, I really believe Darcy does take care of him in those points

261
00:21:11,332 --> 00:21:14,799
where he most wants care. From something that he told me in our journey hither,

262
00:21:14,799 --> 00:21:19,666
I have reason to think Bingley very much indebted to him. But I ought to beg his pardon,

263
00:21:19,666 --> 00:21:23,265
for I have no right to suppose that Bingley was the person meant. It was all conjecture.”

264
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:27,233
“What is it you mean?” “It is a circumstance which

265
00:21:27,233 --> 00:21:30,700
Darcy of course could not wish to be generally known, because if it were to get round to the

266
00:21:30,700 --> 00:21:35,933
lady’s family it would be an unpleasant thing.” “You may depend upon my not mentioning it.”

267
00:21:35,933 --> 00:21:39,966
“And remember that I have not much reason for supposing it to be Bingley. What he told me was

268
00:21:39,966 --> 00:21:44,166
merely this: that he congratulated himself on having lately saved a friend from the

269
00:21:44,166 --> 00:21:48,765
inconveniences of a most imprudent marriage, but without mentioning names or any other particulars;

270
00:21:48,765 --> 00:21:52,500
and I only suspected it to be Bingley from believing him the kind of young man to get

271
00:21:52,500 --> 00:21:56,400
into a scrape of that sort, and from knowing them to have been together the whole of last summer.”

272
00:21:57,532 --> 00:22:00,299
“Did Mr. Darcy give you his reasons for this interference?”

273
00:22:00,299 --> 00:22:03,900
“I understood that there were some very strong objections against the lady.”

274
00:22:04,799 --> 00:22:10,265
“And what arts did he use to separate them?” “He did not talk to me of his own arts,”

275
00:22:10,265 --> 00:22:14,866
said Fitzwilliam, smiling. “He only told me what I have now told you.”

276
00:22:14,866 --> 00:22:20,400
Elizabeth made no answer, and walked on, her heart swelling with indignation. After

277
00:22:20,400 --> 00:22:23,700
watching her a little, Fitzwilliam asked her why she was so thoughtful.

278
00:22:24,666 --> 00:22:34,599
“I am thinking of what you have been telling me,” said she. “Your cousin’s conduct does not suit my feelings. Why was he to be the judge?” “You are rather disposed to call

279
00:22:34,599 --> 00:22:43,433
his interference officious?” “I do not see what right Mr. Darcy had to decide on the propriety of his friend’s inclination; or why, upon his own judgment alone,

280
00:22:43,433 --> 00:22:46,866
he was to determine and direct in what manner that friend was to be happy.

281
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:53,332
But,” she continued, recollecting herself, “as we know none of the particulars, it is not fair

282
00:22:53,332 --> 00:22:57,233
to condemn him. It is not to be supposed that there was much affection in the case.”

283
00:22:57,900 --> 00:23:02,500
“That is not an unnatural surmise,” said Fitzwilliam; “but it is lessening the

284
00:23:02,500 --> 00:23:07,799
honour of my cousin’s triumph very sadly.” This was spoken jestingly, but it appeared

285
00:23:07,799 --> 00:23:12,765
to her so just a picture of Mr. Darcy, that she would not trust herself with an answer; and,

286
00:23:12,765 --> 00:23:21,400
therefore, abruptly changing the conversation, talked on indifferent matters till they reached the Parsonage. There, shut into her own room, as soon as their visitor left them,

287
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:26,032
she could think without interruption of all that she had heard. It was not to be supposed that

288
00:23:26,032 --> 00:23:30,332
any other people could be meant than those with whom she was connected. There could not exist

289
00:23:30,366 --> 00:23:35,500
in the world two men over whom Mr. Darcy could have such boundless influence. That he had been

290
00:23:35,500 --> 00:23:39,065
concerned in the measures taken to separate Mr. Bingley and Jane, she had never doubted;

291
00:23:39,065 --> 00:23:43,200
but she had always attributed to Miss Bingley the principal design and arrangement of them.

292
00:23:43,200 --> 00:23:49,299
If his own vanity, however, did not mislead him, he was the cause—his pride and caprice were the

293
00:23:49,299 --> 00:23:59,866
cause—of all that Jane had suffered, and still continued to suffer. He had ruined for a while every hope of happiness for the most affectionate, generous heart in the world; and no one could say

294
00:23:59,866 --> 00:24:04,900
how lasting an evil he might have inflicted. “There were some very strong objections against

295
00:24:04,900 --> 00:24:10,200
the lady,” were Colonel Fitzwilliam’s words; and these strong objections probably were,

296
00:24:10,200 --> 00:24:14,933
her having one uncle who was a country attorney, and another who was in business in London.

297
00:24:15,466 --> 00:24:21,765
“To Jane herself,” she exclaimed, “there could be no possibility of objection,—all loveliness and

298
00:24:21,765 --> 00:24:27,233
goodness as she is! Her understanding excellent, her mind improved, and her manners captivating.

299
00:24:27,233 --> 00:24:32,200
Neither could anything be urged against my father, who, though with some peculiarities,

300
00:24:32,200 --> 00:24:37,000
has abilities which Mr. Darcy himself need not disdain, and respectability which he will probably

301
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:42,166
never reach.” When she thought of her mother, indeed, her confidence gave way a little; but

302
00:24:42,166 --> 00:24:46,966
she would not allow that any objections there had material weight with Mr. Darcy, whose pride, she

303
00:24:46,966 --> 00:24:51,299
was convinced, would receive a deeper wound from the want of importance in his friend’s connections

304
00:24:51,299 --> 00:24:56,332
than from their want of sense; and she was quite decided, at last, that he had been partly governed

305
00:24:56,332 --> 00:25:01,065
by this worst kind of pride, and partly by the wish of retaining Mr. Bingley for his sister.

306
00:25:01,666 --> 00:25:06,166
The agitation and tears which the subject occasioned brought on a headache; and it

307
00:25:06,166 --> 00:25:10,733
grew so much worse towards the evening that, added to her unwillingness to see Mr. Darcy,

308
00:25:10,733 --> 00:25:19,565
it determined her not to attend her cousins to Rosings, where they were engaged to drink tea. Mrs. Collins, seeing that she was really unwell, did not press her to go,

309
00:25:19,565 --> 00:25:23,866
and as much as possible prevented her husband from pressing her; but Mr. Collins could not

310
00:25:23,866 --> 00:25:28,200
conceal his apprehension of Lady Catherine’s being rather displeased by her staying at home.

311
00:25:30,466 --> 00:25:31,200
CHAPTER XXXIV.
